arly Christianity contains the Gospels as foundational texts that narrate the life and teachings of Jesus."}], "places": [{"name": "Jerusalem, Israel", "role": "Cultural center"}, {"name": "Rome, Italy", "role": "Major influence"}, {"name": "Antioch, Turkey", "role": "Early Christian hub"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Gospel", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "gospel"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q34274", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gospel"], "tags": ["religious text", "New Testament & Christian Texts", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Gospel is remembered as a landmark text of the Classical era, referenced in 163 Wikipedia language editions. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "The Gospels significantly shaped the trajectory of Western civilization by influencing religious thought, leading to the establishment of Christianity as a dominant faith with over two billion adherents today.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-11T13:53:43Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27351656017", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Gospel originally meant the Christian message, but in the second century AD the term euangélion came to be used also for the books in which the message was reported. In this sense a gospel can be defined as a loose-knit, episodic narrative of the words and deeds of Jesus Christ, culminating in his trial and death, and concluding with various reports of his post-resurrection appearances.", "newValue": "The Gospel, derived from the Greek term 'euangélion', signifies the core message of Christianity, initially articulated between 30-100 AD in the Eastern Mediterranean, particularly in regions such as Judea and Galilee. This text is pivotal as it provides both theological foundations and historical accounts of Jesus Christ's life, death, and resurrection, influencing billions across centuries. The authors traditionally attributed to the Gospels—Matthew, Mark, Luke, and John—each narrated the teachings and miracles of Jesus, shaping the beliefs and practices of early Christian communities.

The Gospels were written in a context of early Christian communities facing persecution and the need to establish a cohesive narrative about Jesus' significance. The first Gospel, attributed to Mark, is believed to have been composed around 70 AD, with the others following in the subsequent decades. These texts not only preserved oral traditions but also responded to the socio-political realities of the time, addressing the needs for identity and guidance among early Christians. As they circulated, they facilitated the spread of Christianity throughout the Roman Empire and beyond.

